o sentences below. Does the word have the same meaning in both sentences? Answer with only \"Yes\" or \"No\".\n\nSentence 1: To pick rags.\nSentence 2: Don't always pick on your little brother.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct choice. ","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"No\n\nThe word \"pick\" does not share the same meaning across the two sentences. In Sentence 1 it denotes the act of gathering or collecting (as in selecting or lifting rags). In Sentence 2 the phrasal verb \"pick on\" denotes teasing or bullying. These are distinct senses, so the answer is No.\n\n\\boxed{No}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":201,"output_tokens":72},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.62e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"SuperGLUE-Wic_19751","prompt":"Consider the word \"bottom\" in the two sentences below. Does the word have the same meaning in both sentences? Answer with only \"Yes\" or \"No\".\n\nSentence 1: They started at the bottom of the hill.\nSentence 2: They did much of their overseas trade in foreign bottoms.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct choice. ","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"No\n\nThe word \"bottom\" is used with two unrelated senses. In Sentence 1 it denotes the lowest physical part of a slope. In Sentence 2 the plural \"bottoms\" is a conventional nautical term for the hulls (hence the ships) of foreign vessels. Because the meanings are distinct, the answer is No.\n\n\\boxed{No}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":209,"output_tokens":72},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.78e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"SuperGLUE-Wic_19738","prompt":"Consider the word \"slip\" in the two sentences below. Does the word have the same meaning in both sentences? Answer with only \"Yes\" or \"No\".\n\nSentence 1: Slip into something comfortable.\nSentence 2: My grad