 same meaning in both sentences? Answer with only \"Yes\" or \"No\".\n\nSentence 1: I seem to be misunderstood by everyone.\nSentence 2: There seems no reason to go ahead with the project now.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct choice. ","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The word \"seem\" functions in both sentences as a verb of appearance or impression (i.e., \"give the indication that\").  \n- Sentence 1 uses the common construction \"seem + infinitive\" to convey that a state of affairs appears to hold.  \n- Sentence 2 uses the equally standard (if slightly more elliptical) construction \"there seems + noun phrase,\" which is understood as \"there seems to be + noun phrase\" and likewise conveys that a state of affairs appears to hold.  \n\nBecause the core lexical meaning and the syntactic-semantic role are identical, the answer is Yes.\n\n\\boxed{Yes}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":209,"output_tokens":125},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":0.0001043,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"SuperGLUE-Wic_19953","prompt":"Consider the word \"taste\" in the two sentences below. Does the word have the same meaning in both sentences? Answer with only \"Yes\" or \"No\".\n\nSentence 1: To ask at that particular time was the ultimate in bad taste.\nSentence 2: He got a taste of life on the wild side.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct choice. ","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"No\n\n\\boxed{No}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":93,"output_tokens":8}},"cost":1.689e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"SuperGLUE-Wic_20135","prompt":"Consider the word \"dollar\" in the two sentences below. Does the word have the same meaning in both sentences? Answer with only \"Yes\" or \"No\".\n\nSentence 1: He worships the almighty dollar.\nSentence 2: The dollar coin has never been popular in the United States.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct choice. ","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"The word \"dollar\" is used idiomatically in Sentence 1 to refer to money/wealth in general.  \nIn Sentence 2 it is used literally to refer to a specific unit of currency (the coin).  \nThe two senses are therefore distinct, so the answer is No.\n\n\\boxed{No}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":208,"output_tokens":62},"model_used":"x-ai/grok-4.3"},"cost":7.26e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"SuperGLUE-Wic_19880","prompt":"Consider the word \"act\" in the two sentences below. Does the word have the same meaning in both sentences? Answer with only \"Yes\" or \"No\".\n\nSentence 1: She wants to act Lady Macbeth, but she is too young for the role.\nSentence 2: I started acting at the age of eleven in my local theatre.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct choice. ","prediction":"qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Yes\n\n\\boxed{Yes}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":98,"output_tokens":8}},"cost":1.7539999999999998e-05,"accuracy":null},{"global index":"SuperGLUE-Wic_20050","prompt":"Consider the word \"rehearsal\" in the two sentences below. Does the word have the same meaning in both sentences? Answer with only \"Yes\" or \"No\".\n\nSentence 1: A rehearsal will be held the day before the wedding.\nSentence 2: He missed too many rehearsals.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct choice. ","prediction":"grok-4-1-fast-reasoning","generated_result":{"generated_answer":"Yes\n\nThe word \"rehearsal\" denotes a preparatory practice session in both cases (a wedding ceremony run-through, and repeated practice meetings of any kind). The referents differ but the core lexical meaning does not.\n\n\\boxed{Yes}","success":true,"token_usage":{"input_tokens":208,"o